On Thu, 2020-01-16 at 21:56 -0600, bach...@gmail.com wrote:
> Camille:
>
> I think all of those could work; it helps to remember that despite
> these nifty constructs we have come up with for tracking dependence
> among observations, they are all simplifications of the truth and
> different ones work better for different data sets, often just
> because of peculiarities of different problems.  Sometimes it is a
> matter of resources, in that the “iid” models (either varying
> intercept or nested) tend to stress INLA less than the other latent
> variable structures.  Generally speaking it is a good idea to try
> various different types and see which ones provides the most robust
> fit for your data.  I think looking for the one “right” way is
> unlikely to prove fruitful.
>
> With that said, what Thierry suggests is a good choice. The “ar1”
> and “rw2” models also work well in the “replicate” framework for
> subject-year dependence, and I would try those too.
>
> Jonathan
